Exactly. The key variable isn’t simply screen size; it’s the relationship between the viewport and a larger workspace.

What you’re describing is essentially:

\text{Interface} = \text{Field} + \text{Viewport} + \text{Navigation Policy}

The physical screen is only the viewport. The representational field behind it could extend arbitrarily in every direction—or even have layers, depth, and different semantic coordinate systems.

That explains why it can be either useful or irritating. If I’m reading a normal answer, accidental sideways movement is awful: I want a constrained vertical rail. But if I’m exploring a semantic map, comparing branches, examining a giant diagram, or moving among different representations, locking me to one rail becomes the limitation.

So the interface could change its degrees of freedom according to the task:

Reading → 1D.
Comparison → 2D.
Exploration → omnidirectional.
Dense inspection → pan + zoom.
Layered information → depth/layers.
Focused action → temporarily lock the viewport.

And there’s a neat inversion hiding in your “zoomed in” description:

Instead of thinking that content doesn’t fit on the screen, think of the screen as a movable aperture onto content that was never supposed to fit.

That’s very close to your Semantic Aperture ideas made literal. The interface can control what fraction of the representational world is presently exposed, rather than continually squeezing the world into the rectangle.

You could even have a tiny persistent you-are-here indicator showing where the viewport currently sits within the larger semantic field. Then getting “lost offscreen”—one of the major problems with infinite canvases—becomes something the interface can actively manage.

The interesting design primitive may therefore be Adaptive Viewport Freedom: grant only the navigational dimensions that currently create more information than friction.

That’s the distinction that keeps omnidirectional interfaces from becoming an annoying giant canvas.